 me was not  likable not approachable but I read this  book and my heart actually changed and I  never thought I would say that because  you really put in context guys what  Oprah has said in her 60 minutes because  it's not what what are you doing it's  what happened to you and let's start  with his parents you tell a great story  from the time that he was in  chair his dad is hitting the ball  hitting the ball she's feeding him and  just how that imprinted on his mind and  when Nora says ultimate Tiger mom his  mom was the one who has really had had  the killing machine for him Jeff yeah  and she she came comes to America  doesn't speak much in the way of English  she doesn't know anything about American  culture she marries a man who was  married when he married her and it was  also very unfaithful it was very  insightful and so from the very  beginning she sort of swept into this  into this situation in a relationship  and in a country that's very foreign to  her and then she has one child and when  she has a child it happens right when  her her husband is taking up the sport  of golf and he's basically living in the  garage when he's not at work no and the  baby starts coming in and sitting in the  highchair and watching his dad hit golf  balls and so for her to have time with  her son she sits next to the night chair  and in between hits puts food in his  mouth you know Gerber baby food or  whatever it is and to me that if you  look at formation of this young man it  all starts right there  if you right Tigers inability to show  gratitude apologize or express  appreciation was rooted in this  upbringing yeah his mother pampered him  like a prince his father rarely uttered  the words thank you  or I'm sorry yeah and so there's two  things there one is Earl is a guy I  think who felt like they were entitled  when he recognized that his son had a  talent that no one else had I mean there  were certain things about Tiger that he  just had and then there were other parts  of him that I think were taught to him  by the way he was raised but there's no  question he had talent that was I think  god-given I mean he was born with  certain abilities that were honed by the  way he was raised and but once they got  out on the circuit as a junior golfer  and they're you know the equivalent of  Little League and peewee football for  him was golf tournaments where he's  crushing everyone and his dad just  expected that when they went to people's  homes or the tournaments they were the  best and they should be treated that way  yeah so he didn't see thank you we  appreciate you he never saw that his dad  predicted at a very early age that he  would be the greatest  ever play the game and chosen one that  he was a chilly he even used a frozen  engine that on your shoulders growing up  but the way that he treated him he was  very disparaging of him he would put him  down and they had a code word between  them that if it got too much he was  supposed to say enough and you never  said ever said it I think that's how bad  it got that says a lot actually about  that relationship is interesting because  last year Tiger wrote his own book and  he talked about that language that his  father used which as a parent as I have  two sons and two daughters when I read  that language the first thing I thought  was wow I can't imagine speaking that  way to one of my children and expecting  that that's going to make them do better  but Tiger looks back and reflects on  that now and says it helped make him who  he is on the golf course that's one of  the reasons he's so hard to beat this is  a guy who this is a guy who at his peak  journalists who t lee complained about  was remote was untouchable and  accessible has that changed yes it has  change and I think it changed last year  about this time when he found himself on  the side of the road in Florida with a  one hell of a rock star cocktail of  opioids in his system and I think that  was the that was the bottom that for  Tiger and because of his kids and  because he's now healthy for the first  time in five years but he's spiritually  healthy and emotionally healthy for the  first time I think in his life what why  what's what's I think because he I think  what happened in that Florida Road was a  complete wake-up call for him and I  think now he sees his children he does  not want to have the life that he had  with his parents for his kids and you  say what did you learn about whether  he's used performance-enhancing drugs  well there's a chapter called miracle  workers that I worked on a long time on  and it has the most definitive statement  to date the most authoritative statement  to date from mark Lindsay who is Tigers  chiropractor who says and marks had his  hands on Olympic athletes all around the  world and says he does not believe the  tiger used performance-enhancing drugs  Oh perfect timing you know I doubt Tiger  would read this book but he should it  would be very I think helpful and  enlightening even to him I think it was  really very very very well done  and just in time for the Masters Armen  Kitaen and Jeff Benedict who thank you  Tiger Woods goes on sale tomorrow  wherever you like to buy your books
 

